## VIDEO 13
- ID: 2qMuQZfB0co | Video | Published: 2026-11-12 21:00 UTC
- Title: What I Look For In The Asia Session
- URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2qMuQZfB0co
- Views: 978
- Description (key content, LUCAS — COMPLETE ASIA SESSION SYSTEM):
  BEFORE THE OPEN: He finds the Globex open on the 5 minute — the green doji candle — and marks any prominent wick lows below it, plus nearby highs. "This is how I find my levels. I mark out highs and lows that are nearby, and react to what's in front of me at the open."
  Trades the open right at 6:00. If a smaller timeframe bull or bear flag forms, he takes it to the marked level.
  THE A+ SETUP: A bull flag on the 5 minute: pullback, a 30 point bottom wick, new high in the candle, then a retracement back to VWAP with the 9 EMA and 21 EMA holding.
  Entry around the midpoint of that candle. Target the session highs in sequence — nearest wick high first, then the next, then the next — scaling out a contract at each if in multiple.
  Stop at the wick low of the entry. Even conservative targeting = a 2.5 R trade.
  SECOND EXAMPLE: Multiple bull flags, nothing closed below the EMAs, then a break — long the 960s, bottom wick but the 9 EMA holds, ride to the PM high off the kill zones and pivots indicator he uses daily. Shows settings on screen.
  Both examples sat in drawdown first — 30 points on one, 150 on the other — before delivering.
  NO MAN'S LAND: Gap between first ten minutes of Globex and Asia open. Lucas does NOT trade it. "The volatility kind of dies out, and that's a recipe for disaster — especially if you don't have a lot of drawdown. The spread is very interesting, not all the orders are gonna get filled, so it gaps up very aggressively or gaps down."
  Wait for the Asia open when volatility returns. Don't overtrade.
  CORE INSIGHT: "Sometimes it's a lot slower and lower volatility, so you have to be a sniper with your entries and exits."
  SETUPS SUMMARY: "Bull flags and bear flags, that's the whole thing."
